% ADDENDUM READ AND CLEARED, 2026-08-15 (by the author, not from within this
% repository). It is housekeeping: NO result, claim or conclusion changes. What
% it does is acknowledge a related preprint, "Leak Proof PDBBind" (arXiv, 2023),
% which independently proposed an improved PDBbind split using extra curation
% and leave-cluster-out splitting; the authors frame the two as complementary.
%
% WHAT THAT LICENSES, PRECISELY:
%   SAFE   -- citing this paper for the result that cleaning a split LOWERED
%             previously reported scores. That is the use Section 7.3 needs, and
%             the addendum does not touch it.
%   UNSAFE -- citing it as the FIRST corrected split for that benchmark. It is
%             not, and the addendum exists to say so. We make no such claim.
%
% THE STANDING RULE ABOVE WAS WITHDRAWN 2026-08-17. It read: "cite
% graber2025addendum alongside graber2025resolving wherever the latter appears."
% It is withdrawn because the sentences it applied to made a claim neither item
% supports, and the rule was helping that claim look sourced.
%
% WHAT WENT WRONG. 2.3 and 7.3 both said "two groups independently" corrected
% this benchmark's split, citing the paper and its addendum together. Those are
% THE SAME SIX AUTHORS in the same journal -- an addendum is not a second group.
% The underlying fact is real, and it is in the addendum: a separate group's
% "Leak Proof PDBBind" preprint proposed an improved split independently. But
% that preprint is not in this bibliography and a decision was taken not to add
% it, so the claim had no citable source at all. It was removed rather than
% sourced, which is the direction this project resolves such things.
%
% BOTH SENTENCES NOW CITE graber2025resolving ALONE, for the SAFE use above --
% that cleaning a split lowered previously reported scores. The addendum does
% not touch that use, so nothing was lost by dropping it.
%
% THE ENTRY IS KEPT AND IS DELIBERATELY UNCITED, like dodge2020finetuning.
% BibTeX prints only cited entries, so it costs nothing in the PDF, and it
% preserves a resolved bibliographic record that would otherwise have to be
% rebuilt from the DOI. DO NOT RE-CITE IT to "balance" the graber citation: with
% the two-groups clause gone it carries no claim, and restoring it is how the
% error would come back. It becomes citable only if Leak Proof PDBBind is added
% to this bibliography and the independence claim is made properly, from the
% preprint itself.
%
% READ IN FULL 2026-08-17 (this supersedes the earlier VERIFY note). The drop
% was NOT uniform, which the prose must not imply: the authors' own GEMS model
% held its performance on the corrected split, and GenScore fell by much less
% than Pafnucy. Our sentences survive because they are scoped to PREVIOUSLY
% REPORTED performance -- GEMS is their new model, not a previously reported
% one, and GenScore did still fall. 2.3 says "by margins that differed between
% models" to keep that visible. Do not rewrite either sentence as "every model
% fell" or "performance fell uniformly".
% Fields from Crossref.
@article{graber2025resolving,
  author  = {Graber, David and Stockinger, Peter and Meyer, Fabian and
             Mishra, Siddhartha and Horn, Claus and Buller, Rebecca},
  title   = {Resolving data bias improves generalization in binding affinity
             prediction},
  journal = {Nature Machine Intelligence},
  volume  = {7},
  number  = {10},
  pages   = {1713--1725},
  year    = {2025},
  doi     = {10.1038/s42256-025-01124-5},
}

